{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status","id":1466351,"title":"[New Medieval Merit] Church Status","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-11-02T16:04:40-07:00","id":1466351,"title":"[New Medieval Merit] Church Status","content":"I'm helping to write a Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression </i>that's based on the so-called &quot;Dark Ages&quot; of medieval European history. As such, the Church and its clergy will be a big deal in this setting. Would anyone with a background in medieval studies be willing to take a look and tell me if I've missed anything? I appreciate your feedback and suggestions for making this Merit more playable and more historically accurate.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Status (Church) (\u25cf+)</span></span></b></span><br /> <br /> Special thanks to @monteparnas<br /> <br /> You have some official position of power within the Church. In addition to handling money (of which the Church possesses obscene amounts) or requisitioning goods, services, and servants, you may also have the ability to bless, receive confession and offer absolution, conduct Mass, and even censure or excommunicate, depending on your rank and function within the vast and literally Byzantine hierarchy of the Long Defeat\u2019s greatest \u2013 some would say <i>only</i> \u2013 institution of consequence.<br /> <br /> Each dot in Status (Church) grants you a +1 bonus to all Mental dice pools aimed at navigating the complexities of Canon Law and interacting with Church officials (such as knowing who to bribe for a special dispensation or a private audience with an archbishop) <i>and</i> reduces by one the number of Doors you must open when using Social Maneuvering against your fellow Christians whom you outrank; this bonus does <i>not</i> apply against individuals who have more dots in Church Status than you do.<br /> <br /> A character need not purchase dots in this Merit to rise to a new station within the Church, this Merit only reflects the amount of influence one has. An ordinary but charismatic priest might have many dots in this Merit to reflect being the figurehead of a powerful social movement (e.g. St Francis of Assisi, or Savonarola), while a weak-willed pope who won his office by being a compromise candidate might only have a couple dots in this Merit. Likewise, females can have more influence than their status as mere nuns or Mothers Superior would normally confer, to represent being secretary to a powerful archbishop or having some other form of official power which does not confer spiritual authority (e.g. being in charge of Church finances while still a mere deaconess).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Gaining a second dot in Church Status has an additional requirement: the character must have a dot in either Language (Latin) or Language (Greek), While not a prerequisite, a dot in Language (French) might also be useful. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf: You are either lay officer of the Church (such as </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">deacon or deaconess, vicar, cleric, sacristan, presbyter, etc)</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> or a seminarian training for the clergy. Though you cannot perform any of the Holy Sacraments, you have limited access to Church grounds, buildings, records, relics, and personnel. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf: You are an ordained minister of the Church (e.g. </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">priest, friar, chaplain, nun, monk, scribe, or other </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">). Males can perform the Holy Sacraments: baptism, confirmation, communion, penance, holy orders, and anointing the sick or last rites, but cannot enter female-only grounds without being specifically authorized by the Mother Superior. You may live and eat on church grounds for free, although you may and will be required to work. </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Females cannot perform any of the Holy Sacraments, and cannot personally (but may indirectly) gain more dots in Church Status than this\u2026 at least officially. In early Christian Ireland, there are tales of female priests and bishops like St Brigid of Kildare who give communion, take confession, and even celebrate mass \u2013 a practice which is considered heretical by the Church proper, and will be stamped out just as soon as the continental bishops get wind of it.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf: You are the main priest of a single parish or abbey, or the Mother Superior of a nunnery. You have administrative control over church properties within it,</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> direct authority over its clerical body,</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> and spiritual authority over its populace. Alternatively you may be a priest directly working for a Bishop, holding greater influence but less authority, or an Auxiliary Bishop (a direct secretary to a Bishop) with minor influence.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">You are a bishop, exert control over a diocese (a collection of parishes), and may perform the most restrict sacraments, the ordaining of priests and the excommunication. Under Canon Law you're now fully equal to an apostle in spiritual authority, being able to settle theological matters for your jurisdiction. In areas where secular authorities are absent you may also assume their role, and even where they're present you usually have the power to indicate judges for both religious and secular disputes. Alternatively you may be an Auxiliary Bishop with great influence or auxiliary to an Archbishop, or you may be an Archbishop with little real influence despite the title.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf: You are an Archbishop. While it is technically only a title of prestige, your </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">archdiocese (a collection of adjacent dioceses, or a single metropolitan diocese).</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> is of exceptional importance and serves as center of an ecclesiastical province, a big collection of dioceses. At the very least those other bishops pay you a huge amount of respect, but in practical terms you have considerable oversight on them. Alternatively you may be a part of the high administration of the Holy See and still be called an Archbishop even without a diocese under you. The Cardinals, the Roman Curia and the Pope are all Archbishops.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":6327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","user":"Super_Dave","date":"limit on female status from \u25cf\u25cf to \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf. 11-15-2021, 12:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-03T03:26:39-07:00","id":1466428,"title":"","content":"It depends when your game is set; like the Medieval era is a long time and things change quite radically over it. For example:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> in an era where religious education and heterodoxy is paramount, holding unorthodox opinions can be very dangerous. </div> </div> </div>For much of the Medieval era this isn't right, and isn't even really possible. Lots and lots of people have all kinds of weird and wacky ideas; it's only really a problem if you're an academic theologian whose publishing stuff (for want of a better phrase). I'm not saying it's not potentially dangerous but that tends to be reserved for mass groups of people, not individuals.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Best of all, you are infallible: nothing that you say or do can be incorrect or contrary to God\u2019s plan, because He would not allow His most trusted and important servant to behave in any way other than the correct way. Your word is more than law, your word is God\u2019s law. </div> </div> </div>From what I understand that's not really how Papal infallibility works, and it's an idea from a much later period. Plenty of popes get in theological arguments with other theologians, and they don't necessarily win simply by being pope.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-03T09:22:28-07:00","id":1466474,"title":"","content":"The Long Defeat takes place during the Early Middle Ages: roughly from the fall of Rome to the Norman Conquest. The setting is centered on France and the British Isles, but we're trying to include at least a little material from everywhere, even outside Europe.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466428#post1466428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Lots and lots of people have all kinds of weird and wacky ideas; it's only really a problem if you're an academic theologian whose publishing stuff (for want of a better phrase). I'm not saying it's not potentially dangerous but that tends to be reserved for mass groups of people, not individuals.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's true for the average person who just goes to church and pays their tithes, but for Geniuses of this era, they're almost guaranteed to come into conflict with the Church and its teachings, one way or another. The average peasant will be educated and corrected: mad scientists can expect a more torches-and-pitchforks type of response.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466428#post1466428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> From what I understand that's not really how Papal infallibility works, and it's an idea from a much later period.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh, I did not know that. Guess I've still got research to do, huh? Thanks for letting me know though. How much authority would the pope have excercised in this period if his authority wasn't absolute?<br /> <br /> On a related note, did medieval bishops have to visit Rome regularly? How often?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-03T13:46:50-07:00","id":1466508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466474#post1466474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's true for the average person who just goes to church and pays their tithes, but for Geniuses of this era, they're almost guaranteed to come into conflict with the Church and its teachings, one way or another. The average peasant will be educated and corrected: mad scientists can expect a more torches-and-pitchforks type of response.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's my point though, for a genius it'd be more that the local synod of bishops demands your presence to answer some questions about something you've written. It's a very involved process that could drag on for years. Plus, it's kinda after the era; like the first universities haven't been formed yet, so the theology of the time isn't necessarily terribly <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">sophisticated, which you really need in order to enforce an orthodoxy.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> I suspect a Genius is probably more likely to just get in trouble for doing something, rather than what they believe or claim.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How much authority would the pope have excercised in this period if his authority wasn't absolute? </div> </div> </div>To clarify, papal infallibility isn't as powerful as it first seems. The problem is that there have been a lot of popes. Papal infallibility would give a huge amount of <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">precedent </span></span></span>that the present pope wouldn't be able to overturn.<br /> <br /> That said, he's still very powerful. He's a secular ruler with the nominal power to excommunicate anyone, and with pretentions to being the ruler of Christendom.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On a related note, did medieval bishops have to visit Rome regularly? How often? </div> </div> </div>Almost certainly not. It's a long trip, and there's not really much need to unless there's specific business.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-03T14:45:16-07:00","id":1466517,"title":"","content":"Papal infallibility was not a thing until the 1870s. Before that the Pope was only first among equals.<br /> <br /> Gotta go have ice cream. Bye!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-03T20:05:12-07:00","id":1466594,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466474#post1466474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Long Defeat takes place during the Early Middle Ages: roughly from the fall of Rome to the Norman Conquest. The setting is centered on France and the British Isles, but we're trying to include at least a little material from everywhere, even outside Europe.</div> </div> </div> </div>All those ideas about the church as extremely unified and powerful, the fires of inquisition and deep control of what's acceptable belief is just not a thing in the whole of early Middle Ages. The church of the time is pretty much adapting to survive instead of being a huge player.<br /> <br /> Our &quot;classical&quot; idea of the Dark Ages is actually mostly from the very end of the Late Middle Ages. As an example, the Inquisitions are mostly a 15th century phenomena, and even then it isn't the Church that actually order the executions.<br /> <br /> The church says you're a bad Christian. The local leaders say that not being a good Christian is <b>treason</b>. The fire was just the common penalty for treason for women, while men were hanged. Things got a lot more complicated, but that was the core from where it evolved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-04T14:16:23-07:00","id":1466703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466474#post1466474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How much authority would the pope have excercised in this period if his authority wasn't absolute?</div> </div> </div> </div>What is often colloquially referred to as &quot;the Dark Ages&quot; is right smack dab in the middle of the period where popes and Franco-German Emperors are in a constant struggle to determine what their respective powers are and who takes precedent. The Emperor says that the pope is a subject, afforded Rome and land around it by royal assent, and while the Holy See has a lot of spiritual authority the final decision making on matters such as appointment of bishops and abbots should rest with the crown (hence placing that hierarchy under royal authority). The pope says that he is God's representative on Earth, charged among other things to have ultimate decision making over who even is a king (an investment that he can withdraw, theoretically liberating subjects from oaths of fidelity to kings), and that through the power to appoint bishops and abbots the Church functions as a hierarchy parallel to but separate from princes and nobles (and hence should get to do things like run its own law courts).<br /> <br /> Hashing out this dispute takes the form of legal argument, diplomatic wrangling, and even proxy war (popes might support people rebelling against emperors) across centuries, and the manner of the divisions is very complicated. Like, you can have an emperor who is fighting tooth and nail to assert dominance over the papacy, and yet when the pope enacts some kind of official condemnation, walks barefoot in a hairshirt for miles to Rome in order to beg forgiveness.<br /> <br /> Clerics of the Church are not quite evenly divided in their loyalties; that is to say, it's not quite as simple as some bishops and priests siding with the pope and some with the crown. There are plenty who are kind of walking the middle ground.<br /> <br /> And you do get some outlying parts of Christendom such as Ireland where a lot of the churches are nominally under the authority of the pope but do a lot of their own things.<br /> <br /> (Also to note, the early Medieval Church was actually the institution that <i>preserved</i> a lot of technical knowledge from Rome and Greece, and had a lot of academia. This kind of thing helped fuel the Frankish Renaissance, the sort of topic which is why modern historians don't really employ the term &quot;Dark Ages&quot;. Which, to be clear, was always a reference in the first place to being in the dark about the period due to reduced record keeping from lowered literacy and less widespread bureaucracy more than any suggestion of it as particularly grim and ignorant.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-04T16:15:22-07:00","id":1466721,"title":"","content":"The term &quot;Dark Ages&quot; was coined during the Renaissance, along with their bigoted fabrications about the Middle Ages. Today it is completely rejected in academia.<br /> <br /> I only see it being used nowadays for the Greek Dark Ages, that is an unrelated period in ancient Greek history, the centuries between the fall of Linear B and rise of Greek writing, and is strictly defined by the lack of written records in Greece.<br /> <br /> Church history is messy, and extremely complex during the Middle Ages. Without the Western Empire to give it legitimacy, the church had to make a lot of complex moves to &quot;exchange&quot; legitimacy with the &quot;barbarian kings&quot;, and had still a lot of internal conflicts that interacted with those external ones.<br /> <br /> Not to say that the very definition of who's the Pope isn't that clear-cut, and Anti-Popes abounded.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-04T19:20:56-07:00","id":1466740,"title":"","content":"I want to point out that levels of authority varied from pope to pope based on things like their force of personality and administrative skill, but I'm not sure how useful that is to translating as a Status Merit...<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the actual mechanical function of the Status Merit is bonuses to relevant Social rolls. So a character who's not very impressive could be highly ranked and still leverage that a bit, but to only generally average results when they're not experiencing a penalty.<br /> <br /> Otherwise, Medieval popes are a lot like kings; there's theoretically a lot of power, but in practice it tends to take an enormous amount of wrangling to actually use it. The typical objectives of such popes are maintaining their prestige among the royalty of Europe, securing territorial sovereignty, and trying to enforce standardised rites and sacraments (that last one being the kind of thing that ultimately leads to the Great Schism).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-05T03:36:11-07:00","id":1466757,"title":"","content":"For the merit itself, I'd maybe consider making archbishop the 5th dot. As far as I can tell, cardinals, whilst already established at this point, aren't as important as they will become, and you generally won't see them outside of Rome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-05T04:33:40-07:00","id":1466760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466757#post1466757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the merit itself, I'd maybe consider making archbishop the 5th dot. As far as I can tell, cardinals, whilst already established at this point, aren't as important as they will become, and you generally won't see them outside of Rome.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. I'd add the Diocesans, Abbots and Abbesses at Rank 3, that was a glaring hole to me.<br /> <br /> In practice, the structure at the time would be roughly something like that:<br /> <br /> 1 - Lay members. They come in a number of official and non-official roles, but are vital to the running of a parish and maintenance of most churches.<br /> <br /> 2 - Members in training. They also have a number of titles and role, and can already perform certain rituals.<br /> <br /> 3 - Priests. Monks, Nuns and Fathers. They can conduct any ritual with a very few exceptions.<br /> <br /> 4 - &quot;Chiefs&quot;. You can have several priests in a single place, but only one is the boss and this matters a lot. They are higher in practical hierarchy and command the actual material resources of the church at a local level.<br /> <br /> 5 - Bishops. They control several churches and abbeys, mainly as an administrative role, but also have a few key decisions like organizing those who conduct specialized rituals like Exorcisms, and they can conduct the rite of Excommunication, that is off-limits below them.<br /> <br /> 6 - Archbishops. Administration of a greater territory, roughly equal to a duchy or a small country. Sometimes more, sometimes less, some have the rank but not oversight over any particular territory, serving internal duties.<br /> <br /> 7 - Cardinals. They are Archbishops with vote on the College of Cardinals, which is the church's Senate of sorts. Nominally their only added role is to vote on the Pope, in practice they have all the influence that comes with that.<br /> <br /> 8 - The Pope. The Patriarch of the Church, whose power is debatable. As in practice he have a lot to navigate, dealing with external negotiations and internal negotiations given the power of the College of Cardinals when it is even stable, which isn't always.<br /> <br /> One interesting note: at the time there's no rule on who can be the Pope except for the decision of the College. The Pope could be selected from anywhere in the Christendom, ordained or lay. On more than one occasion a noble with no previous ties to the church was selected for political reasons.<br /> <br /> The best way to deal with this merit depends a lot on the role the church will have in the game. It may even be a good idea to break it in other merits to cover different aspects: political influence, control over resources, access to rites, political clout to act without oversight, there are many possibilities.<br /> <br /> Some Cardinal may have a lot of political influence and yet no control of church resources whatsoever. Some Bishops can Excommunicate local nobility without questions, while some Archbishops Excommunicate a peasant only with Papal approval.<br /> <br /> Not to mention religious orders, that would require their own Status Merits within the Church.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-05T08:57:29-07:00","id":1466774,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466760#post1466760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Agreed. I'd add the Diocesans, Abbots and Abbesses at Rank 3, that was a glaring hole to me.<br /> <br /> In practice, the structure at the time would be roughly something like that:<br /> <br /> 1 - Lay members. They come in a number of official and non-official roles, but are vital to the running of a parish and maintenance of most churches.<br /> <br /> 2 - Members in training. They also have a number of titles and role, and can already perform certain rituals.<br /> <br /> 3 - Priests. Monks, Nuns and Fathers. They can conduct any ritual with a very few exceptions.<br /> <br /> 4 - &amp;quot;Chiefs&amp;quot;. You can have several priests in a single place, but only one is the boss and this matters a lot. They are higher in practical hierarchy and command the actual material resources of the church at a local level.<br /> <br /> 5 - Bishops. They control several churches and abbeys, mainly as an administrative role, but also have a few key decisions like organizing those who conduct specialized rituals like Exorcisms, and they can conduct the rite of Excommunication, that is off-limits below them.<br /> <br /> 6 - Archbishops. Administration of a greater territory, roughly equal to a duchy or a small country. Sometimes more, sometimes less, some have the rank but not oversight over any particular territory, serving internal duties.<br /> <br /> 7 - Cardinals. They are Archbishops with vote on the College of Cardinals, which is the church's Senate of sorts. Nominally their only added role is to vote on the Pope, in practice they have all the influence that comes with that.<br /> <br /> 8 - The Pope. The Patriarch of the Church, whose power is debatable. As in practice he have a lot to navigate, dealing with external negotiations and internal negotiations given the power of the College of Cardinals when it is even stable, which isn't always.<br /> <br /> One interesting note: at the time there's no rule on who can be the Pope except for the decision of the College. The Pope could be selected from anywhere in the Christendom, ordained or lay. On more than one occasion a noble with no previous ties to the church was selected for political reasons.<br /> <br /> The best way to deal with this merit depends a lot on the role the church will have in the game. It may even be a good idea to break it in other merits to cover different aspects: political influence, control over resources, access to rites, political clout to act without oversight, there are many possibilities.<br /> <br /> Some Cardinal may have a lot of political influence and yet no control of church resources whatsoever. Some Bishops can Excommunicate local nobility without questions, while some Archbishops Excommunicate a peasant only with Papal approval.<br /> <br /> Not to mention religious orders, that would require their own Status Merits within the Church.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pope Alexander VI (Rodrigo Borja) actually had legitimate children before he was elected Pope.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-05T13:07:27-07:00","id":1466810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466774#post1466774\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pope Alexander VI (Rodrigo Borja) actually had legitimate children before he was elected Pope.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know, but he wasn't the only one. And some Popes made more children they freely recognized while being Popes.<br /> <br /> Then you get schisms in the College and two Popes being elected, so to speak, what is called an Anti-Pope.<br /> <br /> And sometimes the schism is so big that two Colleges are formed and each declare their own Pope.<br /> <br /> Finally, that time when the papacy was in France, but not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-05T21:42:32-07:00","id":1466892,"title":"","content":"My notes:<br /> <br /> 1. I'm suspicious of using Status dots to indicate access to wealth. This basically makes it impossible to have high-status low-wealth individuals. Access to places, especially after hours, certainly. An amount of authority and the permission to legally conduct rites, absolutely. But wealth per se, I kinda look askance at.<br /> <br /> 2. I'm a little weirded out by Status being granted via blackmail over an actual official. Like, okay, I find pictures of the Pope doing the nasty with a courtesan, and suddenly I have 6 new dots? Buh? It's one thing to be able to ask a high-Status individual to do your bidding, but quite another to have the Status for yourself.<br /> <br /> 3. I was gonna bring up Abbot/Abbess, but others have already. Monastic Orders are pretty important, overall, as they're essentially the &quot;fringe&quot; elements of the Church that remain explicitly faithful. They're probably more important than the Curia, in terms of how a Genius would interact with the Church. (The Curia is probably better suited to being modeled as a setting element and subsystem than buyable dots.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-06T09:56:39-07:00","id":1466953,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466892#post1466892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My notes:</div> </div> </div> </div>1 - I agree that Status can't be confused with wealth, especially because some of those High Status church members have vows of poverty.<br /> <br /> But it is relevant to bear in mind when they have control over church assets. It isn't the same as being wealthy, but is a thing. An Abbot/Abbess have administrative power over the Abbey and its material resources. You can easily decide that those with sufficient clout to use those resources as they please should represent this by the Resources Merit, but even without that they have command over the Abbey itself, so deciding how the Abbey applies those is clearly within their powers.<br /> <br /> 2 - I don't think blackmail should be the most effective way to have &quot;higher than your place&quot; Status, yet personal influence is a huge thing and it is even greater at the church of the time. Depending on personal alliances and how much respect you command from a big number of people or from key important characters, it is totally reasonable to have a higher effective Status Merit than your position in church hierarchy.<br /> <br /> This was especially true at the higher levels, as the political power became more blurred from Bishop up. But even at lower status things could get &quot;interesting&quot; within 1 level of difference. If the Diocesan fancies a lay member more than his direct subordinates, then here we are.<br /> <br /> 3 - Agreed, although I'm not sure if Monastic Orders are so &quot;fringe&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-09T14:08:02-08:00","id":1467292,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466508#post1466508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's my point though, for a genius it'd be more that the local synod of bishops demands your presence to answer some questions about something you've written. It's a very involved process that could drag on for years. Plus, it's kinda after the era; like the first universities haven't been formed yet, so the theology of the time isn't necessarily terribly <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">sophisticated, which you really need in order to enforce an orthodoxy.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> I suspect a Genius is probably more likely to just get in trouble for doing something, rather than what they believe or claim.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is definitely likely. Do you think, then, that there shouldn't be any drawback to making clergy your beholden? I was trying to limit the players' ability to get around Jabir, but maybe that's unnecessary, and I should just treat clergy like any high-status Beholden: useful, but not especially dangerous if you know how to placate them and keep them out of the really dangerous parts of your laboratory.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466508#post1466508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> papal infallibility isn't as powerful as it first seems.</div> </div> </div> </div>Alright, I've modified the text of the 6th dot to reflect that popes were not yet infallible. Thanks for pointing that out. <br /> <br /> I just wanted to give players and Storytellers a rough idea of the amount of money that a given Church officer would be allowed to handle. As always, having money to spare would fall under the Resources Merit. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466760#post1466760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> In practice, the structure at the time would be roughly something like this:</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a VERY detailed hierarchy! Thank you so much, I feel like that really helps how the Church's various officers and agents would have operated in this era. However, with eight dots available it changes the equation slightly. I originally tried to limit it to six dots because I figured, at least in theory, that some extremely stubborn and willful individuals could potentially resist orders given by the pope by virtue of having a lot of dots in Resolve (and by extension, lots of Doors to open). With eight dots available, if we keep the &quot;removes one Door&quot; mechanic then the Pope will basically have the authority to order anyone to do anything without even rolling for it. Maybe I should allow characters to add their Status to Doors when resisting social maneuvers, since status and privilege were very obvious and important in the Middle Ages, and kings often did resist the authority of the Church (and vice versa)? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466760#post1466760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The best way to deal with this merit depends a lot on the role the church will have in the game. It may even be a good idea to break it in other merits to cover different aspects: political influence, control over resources, access to rites, political clout to act without oversight, there are many possibilities.<br /> <br /> Some Cardinal may have a lot of political influence and yet no control of church resources whatsoever. Some Bishops can Excommunicate local nobility without questions, while some Archbishops Excommunicate a peasant only with Papal approval.<br /> <br /> Not to mention religious orders, that would require their own Status Merits within the Church.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure I want to break this Merit down even further, because A) I'm not sure most players care that much about whether it's &quot;realistic&quot; that a given character has certain ecclesiastical abilities and not others, and B) that can probably be handled on an ad hoc basis by the Storyteller and/or the player. Plus, most of the other uses you've described are already covered by other Merits like Resources, Mentor, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-09T20:29:08-08:00","id":1467319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467292#post1467292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's a VERY detailed hierarchy! Thank you so much, I feel like that really helps how the Church's various officers and agents would have operated in this era. However, with eight dots available it changes the equation slightly.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's more to understand the actual hierarchy than to represent it with their individual dots. In actual game play the Pope, as an example, is likely to not even be relevant unless the game takes place near his lands.<br /> <br /> I recommend that you make a number of changes and simplifications. First of all limit the Merit to what it is. Status is about official power, not underhanded influence, and use of the institutional machine instead of just its political influence.<br /> <br /> A) Forget about wealth. Status is not wealth, Resources is. By equating the rank to wealth you're giving an erroneous representation of what it actually entails. And as much as church authority could be and were used for wealth accumulation, members on all ranks at the time could be rich or poor;<br /> <br /> B) Unless you want to be very lenient, learning Latin pretty much is a requirement for acquiring dots beyond the first. Indeed, it is the most likely distinction between a lay member and a priest in training;<br /> <br /> C) Drop the orthodoxy ball. Although unorthodox ideas may indeed affect face and whatnot, they hardly lead to serious consequence at the time even for the clergy themselves. Instead, they are just quirks or lead to the formation of orders. At the time only very serious heresies that effectively threaten the social order are hunted down;<br /> <br /> \u200bD) Just as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2758\" data-vbnamecard=\"2758\">Errol216</a> said, also drop the blackmail and other unhanded control methods, <b>this is not how Status works</b>. If you have this, you actually have a Retainer or something, and then <i>that</i> character has Status. That's because Status always means work through the official channels of the organization;<br /> <br /> E) Give something for female Status holders to compensate for the things they can't do. The actual church is sexist, the rules shouldn't, as the player pays the same amount of points. And remember, a woman can reach the role of Abbess or other Mother Superior, or may work as direct secretary to an Archbishop. So although the title &quot;stops&quot; at Mother Superior, there is no reason to limit Status level;<br /> <br /> F) Let fine differences be represented by other Merits if the player want them, like Staff, Fame and others. Especially roles as Cardinal or Pope should be left outside normal Status, as they carry less weight by themselves and more by the circumstances around them.<br /> <br /> Suggestion for an alternative:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">: You are either lay officer of the Church or training for the clergy. Though you cannot perform any of the Holy Sacraments, you have limited access to Church grounds, buildings, records, relics, and personnel.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf: You are an ordained minister of the Church. Males can perform the Holy Sacraments: baptism, confirmation, communion, penance, holy orders, and anointing the sick or last rites, but cannot enter female-only grounds without being specifically authorized by the Mother Superior. You may live and eat on church grounds for free, although you may and will be required to work.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf: You are the main priest of a parish or abbey. You have administrative control over church properties in it,</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> direct authority over its clerical body</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> and spiritual authority over its populace. Alternatively you may be a priest directly working for a Bishop, holding greater influence but less authority, or an Auxiliary Bishop (a direct secretary to a Bishop) with minor influence.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">You are a bishop, exert control over a diocese (a collection of parishes), and may perform the most restrict sacraments, the ordaining of priests and the excommunication. Under Canon Law you're now fully equal to an apostle in spiritual authority, being able to settle theological matters for your jurisdiction. In areas where secular authorities are absent you may also assume their role, and even where they're present you usually have the power to indicate judges for both religious and secular disputes. Alternatively you may be an Auxiliary Bishop with great influence or auxiliary to an Archbishop, or you may be an Archbishop with little real influence despite the title.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf: You are an Archbishop. While it is technically only a title of prestige, your diocese is of exceptional importance and serves as center of an ecclesiastical province, a big collection of dioceses. At the very least those other bishops pay you a huge amount of respect, but in practical terms you have considerable oversight on them. Alternatively you may be a part of the high administration of the Holy See and still be called an Archbishop even without a diocese under you. The Cardinals, the Roman Curia and the Pope are all Archbishops.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-10T14:35:00-08:00","id":1467380,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467292#post1467292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That is definitely likely. Do you think, then, that there shouldn't be any drawback to making clergy your beholden? I was trying to limit the players' ability to get around Jabir, but maybe that's unnecessary, and I should just treat clergy like any high-status Beholden: useful, but not especially dangerous if you know how to placate them and keep them out of the really dangerous parts of your laboratory.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, the impression I get is that most people, at least in Western Europe, don't have a terribly detailed understanding of their faith (or maybe more accurately, no one really corrects them). So jabir is going to be a pretty minor issue when even the mundane clergy have access to knowledge not widely know to the lay population.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-10T18:40:27-08:00","id":1467400,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466892#post1466892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. I'm suspicious of using Status dots to indicate access to wealth. This basically makes it impossible to have high-status low-wealth individuals. Access to places, especially after hours, certainly. An amount of authority and the permission to legally conduct rites, absolutely. But wealth per se, I kinda look askance at.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good argument, and one that <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> mentioned as well. Alright, I'll remove the wealth component from the Merit. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1466892#post1466892\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. I'm a little weirded out by Status being granted via blackmail over an actual official. Like, okay, I find pictures of the Pope doing the nasty with a courtesan, and suddenly I have 6 new dots? Buh? It's one thing to be able to ask a high-Status individual to do your bidding, but quite another to have the Status for yourself.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mainly did that so that female PCs could still get dots in Church Status. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar134_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"134\" data-vbnamecard=\"134\">The Kings Raven</a> didn't want to limit the Merit to male characters only, which I can understand from a modern perspective and a gaming perspective, but it's hard to reconcile historically. The medieval Church was openly sexist and extremely rigid in its misogyny. It's hard to reconcile historical reality with our desire to make a game that people actually want to play. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467319#post1467319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> limit the Merit to what it is. Status is about official power, not underhanded influence, and use of the institutional machine instead of just its political influence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then how do we square with the fact that medieval women couldn't gain much status in the Church, but modern female players (and males playing as females) will probably want to?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467319#post1467319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Unless you want to be very lenient, learning Latin pretty much is a requirement for acquiring dots beyond the first. Indeed, it is the most likely distinction between a lay member and a priest in training;</div> </div> </div> </div>It definitely should be a prerequisite, I just wasn't sure about increasing the cost of the Merit by requiring a second Merit to qualify for it. Then again, some Merits do have Attribute prerequisites... <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467319#post1467319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">C) Drop the orthodoxy ball. Although unorthodox ideas may indeed affect face and whatnot, they hardly lead to serious consequence at the time even for the clergy themselves. Instead, they are just quirks or lead to the formation of orders. At the time only very serious heresies that effectively threaten the social order are hunted down;</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467319#post1467319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">E) Give something for female Status holders to compensate for the things they can't do. The actual church is sexist, the rules shouldn't, as the player pays the same amount of points. And remember, a woman can reach the role of Abbess or other Mother Superior, or may work as direct secretary to an Archbishop. So although the title &quot;stops&quot; at Mother Superior, there is no reason to limit Status level;</div> </div> </div> </div>So females might have unofficial power and influence within the Church, not tied to their actual role within it? I thought that's what I was doing when I added the stuff about being or controlling a higher-status individual: giving them other ways to access and influence the Church.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467319#post1467319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Suggestion for an alternative:</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually <i>really</i> like your suggestion for the rewrite. Is it okay if we use it as the actual write-up for the Merit? Would you like to be credited for it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-10T20:46:11-08:00","id":1467411,"title":"","content":"ARGH. I wrote a response, and then I accidentally clicked on &quot;Help&quot; instead of &quot;Post Reply&quot; and lost the whole thing. As a result, the following is gonna be a lot more terse and curt than intended, because I'm annoyed now.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467400#post1467400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mainly did that so that female PCs could still get dots in Church Status. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar134_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"134\" data-vbnamecard=\"134\">The Kings Raven</a> didn't want to limit the Merit to male characters only, which I can understand from a modern perspective and a gaming perspective, but it's hard to reconcile historically. The medieval Church was openly sexist and extremely rigid in its misogyny. It's hard to reconcile historical reality with our desire to make a game that people actually want to play.<br /> [...]<br /> <br /> Then how do we square with the fact that medieval women couldn't gain much status in the Church, but modern female players (and males playing as females) will probably want to?</div> </div> </div> </div>I figured that was the goal, and I agree that the dots should be available to female characters, but I don't think the way you went about it succeeded.<br /> <br /> Instead, I'd suggest writing a sidebar on the subject. The way I see it, a Storyteller has two options:<br /> 1. Screw historical realism for this specific aspect. Do they actually care about having historically accurate sexism? If not, then just declare that, yes, a large chunk of the clergy is female, even at the upper echelons. It's simply not important to keep the sexism unless you're specifically telling a story about that sexism. Obviously, this won't work for tables where people care about historical accuracy, so...<br /> <br /> 2. Provide some guidance about how to change canon law. I.e., how would your female PC become the first female priest, bishop, or pope? What would they have to contend with? Maybe they'd have to convince the Pope, or a specific subset of attendees to an ecumenical council. What would that look like? In other words, the PC can buy the dots, but only after changing the course of history. Which I think is fitting for Genius.<br /> <br /> This way, the Church Status merit can simply reflect official rank and resultant powers and not worry about trying to capture half a dozen other details at the same time.<br /> <br /> Neither of these options mean that you <i>have</i> to lose overall sexism, either. You can have female bishops and also still burn women at the stake on trumped up charges of hexing, for instance. Sexism is flexible, and needs no help to exist, so if a table wants to fight the patriarchy in the Dark Eras, they still can. All <i>you</i> have to do is not have any rules against a gendered character buying a dot in Status.<br /> <br /> This Wikipedia page on the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordination_of_women_and_the_Catholic_Church\" target=\"_blank\">ordination of women</a> is probably worth a read.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467400#post1467400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So females might have unofficial power and influence within the Church, not tied to their actual role within it? I thought that's what I was doing when I added the stuff about being or controlling a higher-status individual: giving them other ways to access and influence the Church.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think unofficial power and influence is well-modeled by dots you purchase with XP. If the book you're planning to put this into is 2E, that makes it protected by Sanctity of Merits and the ST should avoid messing with it. That's a hard ask for something under the table.<br /> <br /> Instead, you'd want to handle it on a more case-by-case basis. Blackmail, for instance, would be an equipment bonus more than anything else, I think. Or if you're a cardinal's sister and have his ear, then maybe you get a bonus <i>specifically in talking to him</i>, but not really anyone else. (I'm thinking of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina,_Queen_of_Sweden\" target=\"_blank\">Christina of Sweden</a>, here, and how she had a thriving relationship with Pope Clement IX; ... and then he died and Clement X set fire to the memory of that relationship: not something you'd want to model by arbitrarily dropping dots from a PC character sheet.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-11T02:38:34-08:00","id":1467422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467400#post1467400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So females might have unofficial power and influence within the Church, not tied to their actual role within it?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you read carefully, my suggestion isn't about women having unofficial power and influence.<br /> <br /> The nun or sister that acts as direct secretary to a Cardinal isn't at all in an unofficial position or role. She follows all the rules: she can't perform sacraments, she doesn't directly oversee rituals or theological matters, her position in the &quot;spiritual&quot; hierarchy is below a Mother Superior.<br /> <br /> Yet, she <b><u>is</u></b> the secretary to a Cardinal. Her level of access, administrative power and respect are all official and yet considerable enough for Rank 4, or even 5. Any priest is theoretically above her in theological matters, yet a lesser Bishop will probably defer to her wisdom. Her influence over the Diocese or even the Ecclesiastical Province is considerable, especially if the Cardinal is too busy with the added responsibilities of the title, even if it isn't direct.<br /> <br /> By all accounts her power is indirect and inferior. Yet, it is 100% through official channels. Does that means it isn't actually a sexist environment? Not in the least. She probably worked harder to attain that position than any man in a similar situation, and most women in a similar position don't have the same actual Rank without constant effort to stay relevant.<br /> <br /> As a rule of thumb, for sexism reflecting on status, I suggest the rule of up to 2 below. A woman in a given official position in a sexist organization of any kind usually has less leverage than the position nominally concedes. You reflect that by giving a character a lower Status Merit for the position, as she is nominally X, but men right above and below her frequently just ignore her, and even people far below know her pull is limited and will be overruled by her superiors if she really tries, so her actual Rank on sheet is X-2.<br /> <br /> Why not make it the default? Because personal skill, effort and luck play a role in changing things. That goes with Status in general, but even greater for such women. Sometimes they have every bit as much power as a man could dream with. So it stays as a descriptive option, no need to charge anything on the player. If a player wants a woman with as much power as her position can afford, just go with it, if the player wants to roleplay the struggle of being unrecognized just let them buy their effective Rank but occupy a nominally higher position. When creating a woman NPC, do as you see fit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467400#post1467400\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I actually <i>really</i> like your suggestion for the rewrite. Is it okay if we use it as the actual write-up for the Merit? Would you like to be credited for it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Be my guest, I would appreciate the credit and that's all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Errol216</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467411#post1467411\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">ARGH. I wrote a response, and then I accidentally clicked on &quot;Help&quot; instead of &quot;Post Reply&quot; and lost the whole thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel your pain...<br /> <br /> Sexism is a complex phenomenon, and sexism in the distant past even more so. Discussions on the role of women in the church were still far from settled at the time, even for modern standards. Well, tbh almost no discussion in the church was settled at the time.<br /> <br /> And for this reason, exceptions did existed. Women were ordained in a few instances at least, and at least one historical case of Bishopess is still undetermined if it occurred. Such situations were complex matters, scandals at least, but in the still fragmentary and convoluted Church of the time it couldn't be completely barred from happening. And then we have the legend of the Popess, for example, and the open question if a woman passing for man to be ordained would be a valid priest or not.<br /> <br /> Burning on the stake is another matter completely and not an actual thing at the time, for women or men. But sexism have so many ways to express itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-13T07:46:13-08:00","id":1467678,"title":"","content":"Alright, I've deleted all the stuff about Jabir and orthodoxy, and added this to the description (along with special credit for @monteparnas):<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status#post1466351\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A character need not purchase dots in this Merit to rise to a new station within the Church, this Merit only reflects the amount of influence one has. An ordinary but charismatic priest might have many dots in this Merit to reflect being the figurehead of a powerful social movement (e.g. St Francis of Assisi, or Savonarola), while a weak-willed pope who won his office by being a compromise candidate might only have a couple dots in this Merit. Likewise, females can have more influence than their status as mere nuns would normally confer, to represent being secretary to a powerful archbishop or having some other form of official power which does not confer spiritual authority (e.g. being in charge of Church finances while still a mere deaconess).</div> </div> </div> </div>Does that sound about right?<br /> <br /> (P.S. St Francis and Savonarola are good examples but not quite the right period. Can anyone think of charismatic but low-Status religious leaders from the Early Medieval?)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/856697-motor-city-breakdown-a-playable-changeling-setting-for-detroit-michigan\" target=\"_blank\">Motor City Breakdown</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/19D2lK7otRprpHsJz0aZF0WucpMtoEgGQ2ExZJ-ud0a4/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">Devil's Night in the D</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1uFgCI8m4gJ5kg1DneG0Cgu18bmUpe4RxDNDfI824G6M/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">The Magical History Tour</a><br /> Accuracy Consultant on <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/AshesOfTheMotorCity.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Ashes of the Motor City</a><br /> Editor, Compiler, and Senior Contributor to <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OxlnZCafIZtgPMCfikl0DJvWAwZKJDLk6lxh9hFPB1g/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Tenebrous Seas</a><br /> <b>Current Project(s)</b>: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/15v9c9IWq_HOjZoYPWqceTxQ0-z1epdN4pt5F6nMUYpE/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\">We Have Fought the Long Defeat</a>, a Late Antiquity/Early Medieval Dark Era for <i>Genius: The Transgression</i>; <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/12IQd13VQ0kHFY19yUBBkqVA-cKT0gZlByOkgYRkb-Ss/edit?usp=drivesdk\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Just Before the Dawn</i></a><i>,</i> a 1970s PtH Dark Era","edited":{"userid":6327,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","user":"Super_Dave","date":". 11-13-2021, 10:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6327-super_dave","name":"Super_Dave","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6327_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-13T12:08:13-08:00","id":1467703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Super_Dave</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1466351-new-medieval-merit-church-status?p=1467678#post1467678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Does that sound about right?</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty good now, although I would also like other opinions.<br /> <br /> I would just raise the female limit bit from 2 to 3, as that would be the rank of a Mother Superior.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}